2. Possible change-detector tests (medium). The `prompt_text()` phrase test (`test_prompt_text_names_nearest_store_and_read_wake_whole`) can only go red when someone edits the prompt wording. `memory-bank/techContext.md` also says "tests do not pin write-rule wording". Do not assert the store-choice or read-wake-whole sentences on `prompt_text()`. The existing `test_agents_md_starts_with_prompt_text` already locks `AGENTS.md` to `prompt_text()`. The `how_to_text()` test (`test_how_to_text_always_explains_root_path_and_read_wake_whole`) matches the existing phrase-assertion precedent in `tests/test_init.py` for command forms and is kept. Limit it to command forms (`--root`, `--path`, the unconditional presence of both) and drop pure-prose assertions such as head/tail/pager wording. The whole step was not struck because these tests sit inside mixed steps (Unit 4 steps 1 and 3 and the last Test Plan bullet).

3. Root-relative `--path` in `Run:` resolves against `$PWD` (medium, inherited from #103 FR-5). `nap --path foo/bar/baz` works from the git root. It also works from inside `foo/bar/baz`, but only by accident: the walk-up from the nonexistent `foo/bar/baz/foo/bar/baz` passes through the real store. From a sibling directory such as `foo/other`, `resolve_parent` walks up to the root store, so the `nap` reports an unknown id. The spec and plan pin "runs unchanged from the git root" and test only those two cwds. Consider documenting that limit in `docs/user-guide/commands.md`, or adding a test that pins the sibling-cwd behavior as known and intended.

4. `start` outside a repository (low). `main()` computes `shown = _cli_path(args.dir)` before `is_dir()`. The plan replaces it with `_root_path`, which needs the git root. `find_store_parent(Path.cwd())` raises `ValueError` outside a repository. `start missing` outside a repository would then raise a traceback instead of a clean exit 1. Resolve the root first and fail with `not in a repository`, or fall back to the input path when no root exists. The existing `test_start_without_repository_errors` covers only an existing directory, so add a missing-directory case.

5. Predicate wording (low). Unit 2 step 4 says "enforce `args.root != (args.path is not None)`", which is the valid-input condition. Unit 3 step 4 says "check `args.root == (args.path is not None)`", which is the error condition. Both are correct but easy to invert. Name the error condition in both places.

6. FR-7 scope (informational). The brief says `-h`/`--help` "anywhere in `argv`". The plan scans only tokens before `--`, so `note --root -- --help` records the literal text. This is a deliberate, reasoned refinement (progress log, plan Behaviors list) and is consistent with the test list. Confirm the operator accepts it.

7. Radical innovation (advisory, not applied). Make the store choice one helper instead of two copies in `summem` and `surgery.py`. Add `_select_store(args, cmd) -> tuple[Path, Path] | None` in `summem`. It validates `--root` xor `--path`, writes the one-line error to stderr, resolves the parent via `find_store_parent` or `resolve_parent`, and returns `(parent, git_root)`. `main()` uses it for `note`, `nap`, `zoom`, and `recall`. `surgery.py` calls `m._select_store(args, "surgery")` and returns `2` on `None`. This replaces `_store_flag_error` and the duplicated predicate and resolution in Unit 3 with one call. It also gives `Saved to` and `_root_path` the git root without a second walk, and it removes the predicate-inversion risk in advisory 5. The cost is one new helper contract and a small reorder of Units 2 and 3. Nothing else in the plan changes.
